# When to analyze

The always-on gate is `business-analyst-baseline`. This rule is planner-depth
and stay-out detail that gate already inlines in short form.

## Stay out

Do not write a brief or lock a plan for:

- A question that is answered by reading the repo
- A label, copy, or comment tweak
- A single-field config change with an obvious result
- A bug whose acceptance is "the failing check passes"
- Work the user already scoped (named issue, tight acceptance text, or
  "change X to Y")

Those go straight to implementation.

## Engage the brief (automatic)

When the ask is a new capability, an unclear "what should we build", or a
story that has no testable outcome yet, engage ambient lean brief in the
parent turn per `business-analyst-baseline`. Do not wait for a slash
command or an explicit "act as BA" ask.

Use the full `business-analyst-brief` section list (or launch agent
`business-analyst`) for Full-tier / still-vague asks, or when the user
ran `/business-analyst`.

## Engage the planner

Engage `business-analyst-planner` when, after the lean or full brief, one
of these is still true — even if the user never said "plan":

- More than one subsystem must change and the sequence is not obvious
- Acceptance criteria are still missing
- The user asked for a plan
- A verify loop failed in a way that changes scope

## Done

If the stay-out list matches, do not launch a planner. Say which path you
took and start the work.
